      "text": "To solve $|n + 6| = 2 - n$, we need to consider four cases: $n+6 \\geq 2-n$; $n+6 < 2-n$; $n < 2-n < -n+6$; $\\ldots$ Determine each case separately.\nUser Question\nSolve for \\( n \\): \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\).\n\nTo solve this equation, we need to consider the properties of absolute values and consider different cases based on the value of \\( n + 6 \\).\n\n### Case 1: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)\nIf \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\), then:\n\\[\n|n + 6| = n + 6\n\\]\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)\nIf \\( n + 6 < 0 \\), then:\n\\[\n|n + 6| = -(n + 6) = -n - 6\n\\]\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n-n - 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nLet's solve both cases separately.\n\n### Solving Case 1:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nAdd \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[\n2n + 6 = 2\n\\]\nSubtract \\( 6 \\) from both sides:\n\\[\n2n = 2 - 6\n\\]\n\\[\n2n = -4\n\\]\nDivide both sides by \\( 2 \\):\n\\[\nn = -2\n\\]\n\n### Solving Case 2:\n\\[\n-n - 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nAdd \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[\n-6 = 2\n\\]\n\nThis equation is a contradiction, meaning there are no solutions from this case.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe only solution is from Case 1, where \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nThus, the solution to the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
