      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(|n + 6| = 2 - n\\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The absolute value of a number \\(x\\), denoted \\(|x|\\), is the non-negative value of \\(x\\) or the additive inverse of \\(x\\). Therefore, we can break the absolute value equation into two cases:\n\n1. \\(n + 6 = 2 - n\\)\n2. \\(n + 6 = -(2 - n)\\)\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\n**Case 1: \\(n + 6 = 2 - n\\)**\n\n1. Add \\(n\\) to both sides of the equation:\n   \\[\n   n + 6 + n = 2 - n + n\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the equation gives:\n   \\[\n   2n + 6 = 2\n   \\]\n2. Subtract 6 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   2n + 6 - 6 = 2 - 6\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further gives:\n   \\[\n   2n = -4\n   \\]\n3. Divide both sides by 2:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2n}{2} = \\frac{-4}{2}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying gives:\n   \\[\n   n = -2\n   \\]\n\n**Case 2: \\(n + 6 = -(2 - n)\\)**\n\n1. Simplify the right side:\n   \\[\n   n + 6 = -2 + n\n   \\]\n2. Subtract \\(n\\) from both sides:\n   \\[\n   n + 6 - n = -2 + n - n\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the equation gives:\n   \\[\n   6 = -2\n   \\]\n3. This is a contradiction, so there is no solution from this case.\n\nFrom the above steps, we see that the only solution is \\(n = -2\\). To verify, we substitute \\(n = -2\\) back into the original equation:\n\\[\n| -2 + 6 | = 2 - (-2)\n\\]\n\\[\n| 4 | = 2 + 2\n\\]\n\\[\n4 = 4\n\\]\nSince both sides are equal, our solution is consistent. Therefore, the solution to the equation is \\(\\boxed{-2}\\).",
