      "text": "To solve the equation \\(|n + 6| = 2 - n\\), we need to consider the definition of absolute value and break it down into two cases based on the value of \\(n\\).\n\n**Case 1:** \\((n + 6) \\geq 0\\)\n\nIn this case, \\(n + 6\\) is non-negative, and the absolute value function simplifies to:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nTo solve for \\(n\\), we first add \\(n\\) to both sides of the equation:\n\\[\nn + n + 6 = 2 - n + n \\implies 2n + 6 = 2\n\\]\nNext, we subtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[\n2n + 6 - 6 = 2 - 6 \\implies 2n = -4\n\\]\nFinally, we divide by 2:\n\\[\n2n = -4 \\implies n = -2\n\\]\nSince \\(-2 + 6 = 4 \\geq 0\\), our solution \\(n = -2\\) satisfies this case.\n\n**Case 2:** \\((n + 6) < 0\\)\n\nIn this case, \\(n + 6\\) is negative, and the absolute value function becomes:\n\\[\n-(n + 6) = 2 - n\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n-n - 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nAdding \\(n\\) to both sides gives:\n\\[\n-n - 6 + n = 2 - n + n \\implies -6 = 2\n\\]\nThis is a contradiction, meaning there is no value of \\(n\\) that satisfies this case. Therefore, there are no solutions in this case.\n\nCombining the results from both cases, we conclude that the only solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
